  "situation": "Participants in the postwar discussions agreed that the country needed an intelligence capability, but disagreed about how centralized it should be. General proposals ranged from a powerful central agency that would dominate departmental intelligence services to arrangements preserving substantial departmental responsibility. The Navy argued that operating departments needed intelligence tailored to their own functions. The Joint Chiefs favored coordination but opposed tight centralization. The Bureau of the Budget developed a compromise directive for the President’s consideration.",
  "question": "What should the President do about allocating authority between a central intelligence group and departmental intelligence services?",
